Moving to map, filter, reduce
These three are in every React codebase. Rendering lists, filtering data, transforming API responses — all of it uses these. Learn them properly now.

map — transform every item

```javascript
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const doubled = numbers.map((num) => num * 2);
console.log(doubled); // [2, 4, 6, 8]
```

Original array is untouched. `map` always returns a new array of the same length.

Example from `day2.js`:

- `users.map(user => user.name)` returns just the names.
- `users.map(user => ({ ...user, isAdult: true }))` returns a new array of objects with the extra property.

Why this matters:

- In React, `map` is how you turn data into JSX lists.
- It does not mutate the original array, so it is safe for rendering and state updates.

filter — keep items that pass a condition

```javascript
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const evens = numbers.filter((num) => num % 2 === 0);
console.log(evens); // [2, 4]
```

Returns a new array, shorter or equal length. Never longer.

Example from `day2.js`:

- `users.filter(user => user.age >= 20)` keeps only adults.
- `users.filter(user => user.name.startsWith("A"))` keeps only names starting with A.

Why this matters:

- Use `filter` to show only items that match a condition.
- In React, it is common for search, sort, and visibility logic.

reduce — collapse array into a single value
This one trips people up. Read carefully.

```javascript
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const sum = numbers.reduce((accumulator, current) => {
  return accumulator + current;
}, 0);
console.log(sum); // 10
```

- `accumulator` is the running result.
- `current` is the current item being processed.
- `0` is the starting value.

Think of it like a snowball rolling down a hill, collecting snow. The accumulator is the snowball.

Examples from `day2.js`:

- `productPrices.reduce((acc, curr) => acc + curr, 0)` calculates total price.
- `products.reduce((acc, curr) => { acc.push(curr.name); return acc }, [])` builds an array of item names.

Why this matters:

- `reduce` can do more than math. It can build arrays, objects, or any single result.
- It is useful when you need a summary or complex transformation from an array.

Chaining `filter` and `reduce`
The last exercise combines both in one line:

```javascript
const inStock = products
  .filter((obj) => obj.inStock)
  .reduce((acc, curr) => acc + curr.price, 0);
```

This means:

1. Keep only products that are in stock.
2. Add up the prices of those filtered products.

Why this matters:

- Chaining keeps code compact and expressive.
- In real apps, you often filter data first, then summarize it.

Specific exercise questions explained

- "Return a new array of just the names": use `map` to extract only the `name` field.
- "Return a new array of objects with a new property added": use `map` to copy each object and add `isAdult`.
- "Return only users who are 20 or older": use `filter` with a numeric condition.
- "Return only users whose name starts with the letter 'A'": use `filter` and string matching.
- "Calculate the total price of all orders": use `reduce` to sum the `price` values.
- "Return an array of just the item names using reduce — not map": use `reduce` with an array accumulator and `push`.
- "Get the total price of only in-stock products — using filter and reduce chained together in one line": first `filter` in-stock items, then `reduce` their prices.

React connection

- `map` is how we render arrays of components.
- `filter` is how we decide which items show up.
- `reduce` is how we compute totals, counts, or transformed results.

Summary

- `map` keeps array length the same and transforms every item.
- `filter` keeps only matching items.
- `reduce` collapses an array into a single value or structure.
- Use chaining when you need multiple steps in a single flow.
